course: medicine
lesson: 31
title: Why we sleep when ill
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When you are ill, a lot of what you feel is not the germ itself but your own body fighting it: the immune system sends out chemical messengers that make you sleepy, achy and off your food.
explain: It is like a city under attack switching off the fairground lights to send all the power to the defenders: you rest, you eat less, and the energy goes to the fight.
explain: The shivers are part of the plan too: the brain turns up the body's thermostat, and you feel freezing until your body has heated up to the new setting, even though you are already hot to touch.
explain: Recovery takes a little longer than the germ: you can still be tired after it's gone, and sometimes a second infection, like an ear infection after a cold, makes you worse again just when you felt better.
fact: When fighting an infection the immune system releases chemical messengers, called cytokines, that make you sleepy so the body can save energy.
fact: Sleeping a lot when ill is not laziness; during sleep the body repairs itself and the immune system works hard.
fact: Children who regularly sleep well tend to catch fewer infections, though sleep doesn't cure everything.
fact: Appetite often drops during an infection; eating little for a day or two is fine, but drinking enough is important.
fact: The aches all over in flu come mostly from the immune system's chemical messengers, not from the virus attacking the muscles.
fact: In a fever the brain raises the body's temperature setting, so you shiver and feel cold until you reach it, then feel hot and sweaty.
fact: Light layers and drinks are better than piling on heavy blankets, which can make a fever higher and more uncomfortable.
fact: Feeling better and then worse again can mean a second infection, such as an ear or chest infection after a cold, and a grown-up should get it checked.
fact: Going back to school too soon can leave you exhausted, because the body is still tired after the germ has gone, and straight after a high fever you may still pass the germ on.
fact: Quiet rest, like reading, drawing or sleeping, helps recovery, and screens late at night make sleep worse.
fact: Comfort from a parent, a cuddle or sleeping nearby, helps ill children feel safe, and needing it doesn't make you a baby.
exercise: Why do you feel sleepy when ill? | Immune chemicals make you sleepy to save energy for the fight | The germ eats your energy directly | Medicine always makes you sleepy | You are bored at home
exercise: Is sleeping all day when ill lazy? | No, the body repairs itself and fights germs in sleep | Yes, you should stay as active as you normally are | Yes, it slows recovery because the blood flows too slowly | Only for grown-ups, children should be up and about
exercise: What do the immune system's chemical messengers cause? | Sleepiness, aches and less appetite | Extra energy and a really big appetite | Stronger muscles for fighting the germs | Better eyesight and sharper hearing
exercise: Is it OK to eat very little for a day or two when ill? | Yes, but you must keep drinking | No, you must eat three full meals a day | No, eat double the food to fight germs | Yes, and drink nothing too, to starve the germ
exercise: What mostly causes aching all over in flu? | The immune system's chemical messengers | The virus slowly chewing on your muscles | Lying in bed for far too long | Your bones growing
exercise: Why do you shiver when you have a fever? | The brain raised the temperature setting, so you feel cold until you reach it | You are really freezing inside, even though your skin feels very hot to touch | The germ is cold | The room is always too cold
exercise: What's better for a feverish child than heavy blankets? | Light layers and drinks | A hot bath and three duvets | A thick winter coat and a hat | Running outside to cool off
exercise: You felt better then worse again. What might it be? | A second infection, like an ear infection | The first germ growing back twice as big and twice as strong | Nothing, it's all in your head | Drinking far too much water
exercise: Why can going back to school too soon make you feel rubbish? | The body is still tired after the germ has gone | School gives you exactly the same germ back again | Teachers give extra homework to catch up | It never does, school makes you well
exercise: Does sleep cure everything? | No, but it helps the body recover | Yes, every single illness there is | Yes, if you sleep for 20 hours a day | It does nothing at all for illness
exercise: Do children who sleep well catch fewer infections? | They tend to, yes | No, sleep makes no difference at all | No, they catch more, because they are weaker | Only in summer, when nights are short
exercise: Which is the best activity on a sick day? | Quiet rest like reading or sleeping | A football match to sweat it out | Gaming till midnight under the duvet | Going shopping
exercise: Is wanting a cuddle when ill babyish? | No, comfort helps ill children feel safe | Yes, only babies and toddlers need it | Yes, it slows recovery and spreads germs | Only on weekends
exercise: Is a sick day a holiday? | No, it's for resting and getting better | Yes, it's a day for fun outings with family | Yes, for gaming all night | Yes, for visiting friends who are bored
test: Big bro: "being ill is the germ wrecking you". What's the bigger truth? | Much of what you feel is your own immune system fighting | Germs do everything, the body just sits and waits | The body does nothing | It's all in your head and you can just ignore it
test: Hot to touch but shivering. Gran piles on three duvets "to sweat it out". Better? | Light layers and drinks, heavy blankets can push the fever up | Five duvets, a hot water bottle and the heating on full | A cold bath straight away | Nothing to drink until the shivering stops
test: What would happen if you forced yourself to run around all day with flu? | Recovery would likely take longer and you'd feel worse | You'd sweat the virus out through your skin by teatime | You'd get better faster | Nothing, exercise cures flu
test: "not hungry, can I just not eat or drink at all?" | Eat what you can, but keep drinking | Yes, nothing at all, the germ starves | No, eat a big hot dinner to keep strong | Only drink fizzy drinks for the energy
test: Ellie had a cold, felt fine, then her ear really hurt three days later. What happened? | Probably a second infection, an ear infection | The cold came back much stronger | She caught flu from her pillow | Ears hurt for a few days after every single cold
test: Your mate came back to school the day after a high fever to "prove he's hard". Why's it a bad idea? | He's still tired and may still spread germs | It's not, it proves he's completely healthy | Fevers make you stronger | Teachers will give him a detention for it
test: Off sick, you watch screens till 1am. What's the problem? | Late screens make sleep worse, and sleep helps you heal | None, watching TV counts as rest | Screens kill germs | It makes the fever go
test: Why might a city at war turn off fairground lights to power its defences? How is your body similar? | It saves energy for fighting, like the body making you sleepy and less hungry | The body turns off the heart for a while, so that the legs and arms get more power | The body makes extra energy for play | It isn't similar at all
test: In a game, your hero's health bar fills only when he eats. What's different in real flu? | Rest and drinks matter most, appetite drops on purpose | Eating more always heals you faster than anything else | Sleep drains your health | Shivering fills up your health
test: Mum lets you sleep in her bed when ill. Your brother says that's for babies. Fair? | No, comfort helps anyone who's ill feel safer | Yes, only babies do it | Yes, it slows recovery because you sleep less well | No, but only if you're under five
exam: Identify the principal origin of the somnolence experienced during an infectious illness. | Chemical messengers released by the immune system | Direct consumption of energy by the pathogen | The invariable effect of medicaments | Insufficient stimulation at home
exam: Whereas a febrile patient reports sensations of cold accompanied by shivering, determine the most accurate explanation. | The body temperature is rising towards a raised setting determined by the brain | The body temperature is falling dangerously | The patient is suffering from frostbite | The ambient temperature is excessively low
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning nutrition during a short infectious illness. | A reduced intake of food is acceptable, provided adequate fluids are taken | Full meals must be consumed without exception, in order to sustain the strength of the patient | All fluids should be withheld | Food intake should be doubled
exam: Ascertain the most probable explanation where a child improves after a cold and subsequently deteriorates. | A secondary infection, such as of the ear | Spontaneous duplication of the original virus within the body | Excessive consumption of water | Insufficient use of screens
